tha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6659.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>12th of the 108 Parshwanaths - Shri Kansari Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: The image of Shri Kansari Parshwanath is of white stone, seated in the lotus (padmasana) posture, approximately 13 inches high and 12 inches wide.</p><p>Location: This temple is in Kharwado, Khambhat.</p><p>History: Kansari is an ancient village of Khambhat. In ancient times a large Jain population lived here, and there were many Jain temples. One such temple was the attractive, ancient, and famous temple of Shri Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath. The image of this Parshwanath is charming, incomparable, and powerful. Over time, as the Jain population dwindled, this image of Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath was brought to Khambhat and installed in a newly built temple in Kharwada, Khambhat. Since the image had been brought from Kansari, this form of Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath came to be known as &quot;Shri Kansari Parshwanath.&quot; This ancient and highly influential image fulfils the wishes of devotees who worship it with great faith. It was worshipped for hundreds of years in Kansari as Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath, and is now worshipped in Khambhat as Shri Kansari Parshwanath. Shri Khambhat Tirth is renowned as an ancient Jain pilgrimage site — the omniscient Acharya Shri Hemchandracharya took initiation (diksha) here, and Jagadguru Shri Harisurishwarji Maharaj Saheb spent seven chaturmas (monsoon retreats) here. In Samvat 1739, Shri Haridevsuri of the Sudhmagach lineage composed a hymn of praise to Kansari Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath, which attests to the antiquity of this pilgrimage site.</p><p>Other Temples: Khambhat has more than 74 Jain temples, of which 7 are in Kharwada. Khambhat is home to 5 of the 108 Parshwanath temples.</p><p>Directions: Khambhat is a very popular port city of Gujarat, well connected by rail and road to all other cities in Gujarat. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here, along with a manuscript library holding many ancient books and scriptures. Manilakshmi Tirth is 29 km from Khambhat, and Kheda Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath is 56 km away.</p><p>Scripture References: Kansari Parshwanath is mentioned in the &quot;Shri Vijaydev Surya Ras,&quot; the &quot;365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,&quot; the &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; the &quot;Shri Bhatewa Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;108 Nam Garbhit Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;108 Nam Garbhit Shri Gondiji Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Shri Kansari Parshwanath Bhashya,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Nammala,&quot; the &quot;Tirthamala,&quot; and the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaitya Paripati,&quot; among others. An image of Shri Kansari Parshwanath is also found in the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai, in the Jiravala Parshwanath temple at Jiravali, and in the Kareda Parshwanath temple.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Kansari Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Kansari Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Mandir, Kharwada, Post: Khambhat, District: Kheda, Gujarat - 388620, India. Phone: 02698-226146, 02698-227276, Mobile: 9327782347.</p><p>(What follows is the continuing narrative of the previous births of Lord Parshwanath.)</p><p>Seventh Birth - Graiveyak God and the Seventh Hell: The soul of Marubhuti, after living as the royal sage Vajranabh, attained kaaldharma (death in a state of spiritual discipline) and was reborn as a god in the middle Graiveyak heaven, while the hunter (the soul that would become Kamath) died and went to the seventh hell. The soul of Marubhuti rose upward like the mercury in a summer thermometer, while the soul of Kamath rolled downward like a ball from a mountain, reaching the very depths of the seventh hell. Such is the strange working of karma — that the death of Marubhuti&#x27;s soul comes about through its encounter with the soul of Kamath, and Kamath becomes the instrument of that death.</p><p>Eighth Birth - Suvarnabahu the Chakravarti and the Lion: When his lifespan in the Graiveyak heaven was complete, the soul of Marubhuti was born as a son to Sudarshana, wife of King Vajrabahu, in the village of Surapur in the eastern Mahavideha region. Sudarshana saw fourteen great dreams (mahasvapnas) at night, which foretold that her son would become a Chakravarti (universal monarch). When he came of age, Vajrabahu was crowned king, and the king and queen embraced the path of monastic discipline, in due course attained omniscience (kevalgyan), and reached liberation (moksha).</p><p>One day King Suvarnabahu went to the stables, mounted a swift horse, and rose into the sky, arriving near a hermitage in the forest of Mount Vaitadhya. There, in the ashram of the sage-chief Galav Rishi, he saw a maiden named Padmavati. Galav Rishi told him that, according to the prophecy of a sage, Suvarnabahu would carry off Padmavati and marry her. Suvarnabahu married Padmavati by the gandharva rite. Later, Padmavati&#x27;s stepbrother, the vidyadhara Padmottar, took them to the city of Ratnapur on Mount Vaitadhya, where many vidyadharas gave their daughters in marriage to Suvarnabahu. One day the chakraratna (divine discus) appeared in the armory; with its aid, Suvarnabahu conquered the six regions (khands) and was crowned Chakravarti.</p><p>Suvarnabahu&#x27;s Renunciation: Once, the Tirthankara Lord Jagannath arrived outside the city of Surapur. While listening to his sermon in the samavasaran (divine assembly), Suvarnabahu attained the knowledge of his previous births (jatismaran gyan) and remembered that he had taken monastic vows before the Tirthankara Kshemankar in a former life. Filled with detachment, he crowned his son as king, took initiation from the Lord, and was entrusted to the elder monks. Muni Suvarnabahu quickly became a scholar-monk (geetartha muni), began wandering alone like a lion, and, through the practice of the Vishasthanak austerities and the noble aspiration &quot;May I bring all beings into the fold of the teaching...,&quot; earned the Tirthankara-nama-gotra karma. While wandering, he arrived at Mount Kshirgiri, where, as he stood absorbed in meditation, the soul of Kamath — now reborn as a lion — attacked him in a fit of burning rage, tearing his body with fangs and claws. The muni, maintaining perfect equanimity, ascended to the Vai