n the lotus (padmasana) posture, approximately 13 inches high and 12 inches wide.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This temple is in Kharwado, Khambhat.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: Khambhat has more than 74 Jain temples, of which 7 are in Kharwada. Khambhat is home to 5 of the 108 Parshwanath temples.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: Khambhat is a very popular port city of Gujarat, well connected by rail and road to all other cities in Gujarat. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here, along with a manuscript library holding many ancient books and scriptures. Manilakshmi Tirth is 29 km from Khambhat, and Kheda Bhidbhanjan Parshwanath is 56 km away.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Scripture References: Kansari Parshwanath is mentioned in the \\\\\\\"Shri Vijaydev Surya Ras,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Bhatewa Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"108 Nam Garbhit Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"108 Nam Garbhit Shri Gondiji Parshwanath Stavan,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Kansari Parshwanath Bhashya,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Nammala,\\\\\\\" the \\\\\\\"Tirthamala,\\\\\\\" and the \\\\\\\"Shri Parshwanath Chaitya Paripati,\\\\\\\" among others. An image of Shri Kansari Parshwanath is also found in the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai, in the Jiravala Parshwanath temple at Jiravali, and in the Kareda Parshwanath temple.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Chanting Mantra: \\\\\\\"Om Hrim Arham Shri Kansari Parshwanathaya Namah.\\\\\\\"\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Trust: Shri Kansari Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Mandir, Kharwada, Post: Khambhat, District: Kheda, Gujarat - 388620, India. Phone: 02698-226146, 02698-227276, Mobile: 9327782347.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"9\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"(What follows is the continuing narrative of the previous births of Lord Parshwanath.)\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-014\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"1wnfg3\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-016\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Somchintamani Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"mthhwq\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}]]}]]}]\\n1f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"footer\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Original source: Rajendra Kumar Jain, Parshv Sadan, Parshv Nagar, Dungarpur\\\"}]]}]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"24:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"10\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Seventh Birth - Graiveyak God and the Seventh Hell: The soul of Marubhuti, after living as the royal sage Vajranabh, attained kaaldharma (death in a state of spiritual discipline) and was reborn as a god in the middle Graiveyak heaven, while the hunter (the soul that would become Kamath) died and went to the seventh hell. The soul of Marubhuti rose upward like the mercury in a summer thermometer, while the soul of Kamath rolled downward like a ball from a mountain, reaching the very depths of the seventh hell. Such is the strange working of karma — that the death of Marubhuti's soul comes about through its encounter with the soul of Kamath, and Kamath becomes the instrument of that death.\\\"}]\\n25:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"11\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Eighth Birth - Suvarnabahu the Chakravarti and the Lion: When his lifespan in the Graiveyak heaven was complete, the soul of Marubhuti was born as a son to Sudarshana, wife of King Vajrabahu, in the village of Surapur in the eastern Mahavideha region. Sudarshana saw fourteen great dreams (mahasvapnas) at night, which foretold that her son would become a Chakravarti (universal monarch). When he came of age, Vajrabahu was crowned king, and the king and queen embraced the path of monastic discipline, in due course attained omniscience (kevalgyan), and reached liberation (moksha).\\\"}]\\n26:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"12\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"One day King Suvarnabahu went to the stables, mounted a swift horse, and rose into the sky, arriving near a hermitage in the forest of Mount Vaitadhya. There, in the ashram of the sage-chief Galav Rishi, he saw a maiden named Padmavati. Galav Rishi told him that, according to the prophecy of a sage, Suvarnabahu would carry off Padmavati and marry her. Suvarnabahu married Padmavati by the gandharva rite. Later, Padmavati's stepbrother, the vidyadhara Padmottar, took them to the city of Ratnapur on Mount Vaitadhya, where many vidyadharas gave their daughters in marriage to Suvarnabahu. One day the chakraratna (divine discus) appeared in the armory; with its aid, Suvarnabahu conquered the six regions (khands) and was crowned Chakravarti.\\\"}]\\n28:T410,Suvarnabahu's Renunciation: Once, the Tirthankara Lord Jagannath arrived outside the city of Surapur. While listening to his sermon in the samavasaran (divine assembly), Suvarnabahu attained the knowledge of his previous births (jatismaran gyan) and remembered that he had taken monastic vows before the Tirthankara Kshemankar in a former life. Filled with detachment, he crowned his son as king, took initiation from the Lord, and was entrusted to the elder monks. Muni Suvarnabahu quickly became a scholar-monk (geetartha muni), began wandering alone like a lion, and, through the practice of the Vishasthanak austerities and the noble aspiration \\\"May I bring all beings into the fold of the teaching...,\\\" earned the Tirthankara-nama-gotra karma. While wandering, he arrived at Mount Kshirgiri, where, as he stood absorbed in meditation, the soul of Kamath — now reborn as a lion — attacked him in a fit of burning rage, tearing his body with fangs and claws. The muni, maintaining perfect equanimity, ascended to the Vaimanik heaven.27:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"13\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$28\\\"}]\\n\"])</script></body></html>","rscData":"","segmentData":{"/_tree":"","/_full":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug/__PAGE__":"","/$d$lang/tirtha/$d$slug":"","/$d$lang/tirtha":"","/$d$lang":"","/_index":"","/_head":""},"headers":{"x-nextjs-stale-time":"300","x-nextjs-prerender":"1","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/la